A||||20||BUhockey#13||Washing the Fiero....||||1||
Z||000000||BUhockey#13||11-11-2001||05:16 AM||||My other car is a Ford Explorer.... any special advise on washing the Fiero?? is soap and water adequete..... can you wax a Fiero???<P>Thankx||||reg||1
Z||000001||JohnF||11-11-2001||09:40 AM||||The Fiero paint really isn't any different than any other car.  Wax/polish it with any good grade of auto refinishing product!<P>Welcome to the forum.||||reg||10
Z||000002||avengador1||11-11-2001||09:41 AM||||By soap I hope you don't mean dish washing soap. This will damage the clearcoat on the car. Use a good quality car wash soap with wax in it (Turtle wax car wash works good). Wash with plenty of suds (foam) and a soft sponge (I use a terrycloth covered sponge), rinse off inmediatly to prevent water marks. When you finish washing the car, rinse it one more time and dry with a chamois cloth to remove the water. You can also use a silicone squeege to help dry the car. You can wax the car, if you want to, with a good carnuba wax paste. Use a separate sponge to wash the wheels. I save the wheels for last because of the brake dust that gets in the water.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Avendgador1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||crypto1079||11-11-2001||11:04 AM||||Um..  car wash?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Formula88||11-11-2001||12:01 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by avengador1:<BR><B>By soap I hope you don't mean dish washing soap. This will damage the clearcoat on the car. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Oops!  I've never heard that.  I use Dawn dishwashing detergent periodically to strip off the wax, then followup with a good quality car wash, polish, wax (using Mequire's 3 step system right now).  Am I ruining my finish?||||reg||9
Z||000005||Sootah||11-11-2001||12:01 PM||||SHHH!!! SHHH!!! You all know better than to answer his questions before he post pics of his car!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Welcome to the forum.<P><B>PEACE<I> -Sootah</B></I><P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Sootah__Nuclear_Happy_Face_Sig.jpg">     <BR><sup><I>Never pull your pants down in Cosco, the dang monkeys will getcha</I></sup> <BR><sup><B>AIM</B>: KSSouter</sup>||||reg||4
Z||000006||Monkeyman||11-11-2001||01:13 PM||||avengador1--I've never heard that either.  I've used dishwashing soap on every vehicle I've ever had.  Even used it on my show truck (big rig--$10k paint job).  No damage to any so far.  Is there a certain kind of dish soap that you've had problems with?  Maybe there's one that has some odd ingredient?  Clue us in.||||reg||1
Z||000007||BUhockey#13||11-11-2001||01:21 PM||||Here it is..... '88 Fiero GT , manual 5-speed, 2.8L....<BR> <A HREF="http://photos.yahoo.com/bc/buhockey_13/vwp?.dir=/My+Photos/Fiero+GT&.dnm=Fiero+GT.jpg&.src=ph&.view=t&.hires=t" TARGET=_blank>http://photos.yahoo.com/bc/buhockey_13/vwp?.dir=/My+Photos/Fiero+GT&.dnm=Fiero+GT.jpg&.src=ph&.view=t&.hires=t</A> <P>------------------<BR>AOL IM: BUhocky13<BR>Yahoo IM: BUhockey_13<BR>MSN IM: BUhockey13@hotmail.com||||reg||1
Z||000008||87GTZ34||11-11-2001||01:47 PM||||BU, I my eyes seem to be going, could you post post your picture a little bigger???<P>MM, I used to use dishwashing detergent on cars but then I read about the newer carwash solutions that were engineered for auto finishes.  Dishes are are a bit more durable and don't show surface deformation as much as cars so I switched.  I also don't use wax on the clearcoat but use the clearcoat sealers.  The water doesn't bead up, but sheets off the car.||||reg||1
Z||000009||rogergarrison||11-11-2001||03:46 PM||||I use Dawn too, for years. My cars are all clearcoat paint, i never wax them, just polish. Dawn really cuts thru grease n grime. But everyone has there own thing i guess. I also always terry towell them dry.<BR>My paint wins me a lot of car shows too.<P>Welcome to the forum too btw. Im sure you see youll get plenty of opinions, some different. But at least you can make your own decision after all the input.<p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 11-11-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||fierospeeder||11-11-2001||03:47 PM||||what ive heard on all the 6 car tv shows i watch is that dishwashing soap will remove the wax.  So its mandatory to apply another layer of wax when your done.||||reg||1
Z||000011||SwissFormula||11-11-2001||04:00 PM||||Hey BUhocky13<P>The car looks like it has 7" wheels in front and 6" rear...<BR>or do i need some new glasses?<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif"><P>Roy<P>------------------<BR>88 Formula, T-Top<BR>1 of 20 Fieros in Switzerland||||reg||5
Z||000012||Nashco||11-11-2001||04:17 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by SwissFormula:<BR><B>Hey BUhocky13<P>The car looks like it has 7" wheels in front and 6" rear...<BR>or do i need some new glasses?<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif"><P>Roy<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>DUDE...BU, your wheels are on backwards!! If you go check it out, you'll see the wheels are different size and offsets.  The two with the really small lip go up front, the two with the larger lip go in the rear.  Go fix it..NOW...lol.  Your car is a spitting image of mine...well, with better paint.  I have the exact same color exterior/interior/wheels and even have the same pinstriping.  I don't like the pinstriping myself, I plan on getting rid of it.<P>Oh, as far as dishsoap, I've read it cuts through your wax, never heard anything about clearcoats.  It is supposed to be good stuff if you wax your car, because it gets rid of all the crap on your paint, even old wax.  That way you have a great base to start with if you're doing the full meal deal wax job.<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000013||avengador1||11-11-2001||08:29 PM||||Dishwashing detergent will oxidize your paint, especially if you don't seal or wax it after you wash. I believe this is the reason so many of us have problems with the clearcoat peeling off or cracking. Carnuba wax works like a moisturizer works on your skin except it's for paint. It will work great on clearcoat finishes also, after all isn't clearcoat paint? ||||reg||1
Z||000014||avengador1||11-11-2001||08:42 PM||||Also the worst dishwashing soaps to use are the lemon type ones. These are worse on your cars finish than acid rain.||||reg||1
Z||000015||artherd||11-12-2001||03:21 AM||||Walk into a ferrari dealer, and ask for some of their good wax  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  <P>(don't be afraid of how much it costs, it's worth it. Any 100% pure white (not yellow, that's crap) Carubana wax will do you well, but it's hard to find a truely 100% white anymore.     <P> Do same if you have Mr Mike's leather.)<P>Wash with only one of the carwashes that is made by a waxing company.  They'll get your car CLEANER, and won't damage paint.<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000016||SLYGUY||11-12-2001||03:28 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by SwissFormula:<BR><B>Hey BUhocky13<P>The car looks like it has 7" wheels in front and 6" rear...<BR>or do i need some new glasses?<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif"><P>Roy<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>For anyone who has problems with the link:<P>  <IMG SRC="http://us.f1.yahoofs.com/users/8c09a20f/bc/My+Photos/Fiero+GT/Fiero+GT.jpg?bclhN87Aj1Zj9VzB">  <P><p>[This message has been edited by SLYGUY (edited 11-12-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000017||rogergarrison||11-12-2001||05:38 AM||||On very very very, rare occasions is it the owners actions make the paint (namely clearcoat) go bad. First remember all that paint is almost 20 years old. The factories all had terrible paint problems when they started dabbling in clearcoats. So bad in fact in mid 80's, GM was repainting them for free to original owners. I know because Ive literaly done hundreds of them, in fact still doing them. Repaints that peel are almost always the fault of poor prep in the first place. Overwashing wont hurt, but over waxing will. Everytime you wax a car, you take a small amount of paint off. Its hard to tell with clearcoat since its clear. But wax an older car without clearcoat paint and see what happens  to your cloth...it turns car color. Why, cause its taking paint off. Personaly, Ill keep washing with Dawn and never waxing. Another side note on wax. Water may bead up on waxed cars, but those beads will do far more damage to the paint than anything else except sandpaper. They act like a tiny magnifing glass and actually burn micoscopic holes in clear. Just my nickles worth from a body shop owner.<P>------------------<BR>         <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1234884&a=11878523&p=52990885"> ||||reg||1
Z||000018||Screwie||11-12-2001||07:47 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by rogergarrison:<BR><B>On very very very, rare occasions is it the owners actions make the paint (namely clearcoat) go bad. First remember all that paint is almost 20 years old. The factories all had terrible paint problems when they started dabbling in clearcoats. So bad in fact in mid 80's, GM was repainting them for free to original owners. I know because Ive literaly done hundreds of them, in fact still doing them. Repaints that peel are almost always the fault of poor prep in the first place. Overwashing wont hurt, but over waxing will. Everytime you wax a car, you take a small amount of paint off. Its hard to tell with clearcoat since its clear. But wax an older car without clearcoat paint and see what happens  to your cloth...it turns car color. Why, cause its taking paint off. Personaly, Ill keep washing with Dawn and never waxing. Another side note on wax. Water may bead up on waxed cars, but those beads will do far more damage to the paint than anything else except sandpaper. They act like a tiny magnifing glass and actually burn micoscopic holes in clear. Just my nickles worth from a body shop owner.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>So don't wax at all? Or how many times would be good?||||reg||1
Z||000019||Oreif||11-12-2001||09:49 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by rogergarrison:<BR><B>On very very very, rare occasions is it the owners actions make the paint (namely clearcoat) go bad. First remember all that paint is almost 20 years old. The factories all had terrible paint problems when they started dabbling in clearcoats. So bad in fact in mid 80's, GM was repainting them for free to original owners. I know because Ive literaly done hundreds of them, in fact still doing them. Repaints that peel are almost always the fault of poor prep in the first place. Overwashing wont hurt, but over waxing will. Everytime you wax a car, you take a small amount of paint off. Its hard to tell with clearcoat since its clear. But wax an older car without clearcoat paint and see what happens  to your cloth...it turns car color. Why, cause its taking paint off. Personaly, Ill keep washing with Dawn and never waxing. Another side note on wax. Water may bead up on waxed cars, but those beads will do far more damage to the paint than anything else except sandpaper. They act like a tiny magnifing glass and actually burn micoscopic holes in clear. Just my nickles worth from a body shop owner.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Here is my car with it's 15 year old paint. Aside from the nose (damaged by previous owner) and the Indy scoop the rest of the car in the pics is ORIGINAL paint. I use Turtle Wax car soap, Meguire's Deep Crystal polish, and Meguire's Carnuba Wax. Anyone who has seen my car in person can attest to it's deep gloss look. <P>(NOTE: Since these pics I have changed to a vented hood and Sage's side panels. Which required those panels to be painted. So may car is now 60% original paint) <P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Fiero_SE.jpg">  <P>------------------<BR>             <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg">           <BR><FONT SIZE="1"><B><I>Happiness isn't around the corner...<BR>Happiness IS the corner.</I></B></FONT><p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 11-12-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000020||rogergarrison||11-12-2001||05:02 PM||||Your car looks awesome Orief. Im not a great lover of black, simply because it get dirty so fast. I just got a 'new' black one myself and its going to be painted another color real soon. I bet yours is garaged and not a daily driver, right. Lots of people dont realize that just keeping a car clean adds years and years to it. Keep it up, and itll stay looking like that for 20 more years. I use a spray bottle and spray my car lightly all over with clear water, and just dry it with a towell every couple days, so it really never gets too dirty. If I do get caught out in the rain, it just gets water with a couple capfuls of dawn, dried off and put away. Every couple weeks or so I put a coat of polish or finishing glaze to take out towell scratches and thats it.<P>------------------<BR>         <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1234884&a=11878523&p=52990885"> ||||reg||1
